WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.120
<v Rayyan>In a June 2025 survey of more than five thousand American adults, 62 percent said they interact with AI at least several times a week.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:10.750
<v Waleed Masri>And half of them said they're more concerned than excited.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.060
<v Rayyan>I'm Rayyan.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:13.450
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>I'm Abdulrahman.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:14.676
<v Waleed Masri>And I'm Waleed.

00:00:14.676 --> 00:00:18.120
<v Waleed Masri>This is the last episode of The Evolution of AI Perception.

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:22.848
<v Rayyan>Each of us wrote a research essay about AI today, and we came at it from different sides.

00:00:22.848 --> 00:00:25.750
<v Rayyan>So we'll each present ours, then talk about where we agree.

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:28.210
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Mine is the hopeful one.

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:36.910
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>My essay is about AI's potential, and I start with the person who probably has the most say in where AI goes: Jensen Huang, the head of Nvidia.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:38.540
<v Waleed Masri>The chip company.

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:39.227
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Right.

00:00:39.227 --> 00:00:50.570
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>In October 2026, Stephen Witt, who wrote a book about Nvidia, called Huang "the emperor of A.I." in The New York Times, because the other big tech companies are borrowing money just to buy his products.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:53.400
<v Rayyan>And Huang isn't worried about AI at all, is he?

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:53.929
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>No.

00:00:53.929 --> 00:00:57.384
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>He told Witt that every problem is an engineering problem.

00:00:57.384 --> 00:01:07.870
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Witt noticed that hardware engineers like Huang see AI as maths that can be controlled, while a lot of software engineers see it as something mysterious that's hard to control.

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:09.598
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Witt himself sits in between.

00:01:09.598 --> 00:01:13.590
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>He worries that understanding AI is years behind making it smarter.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.100
<v Waleed Masri>So what does the good future actually look like?

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:21.057
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Reed Hastings, who co-founded Netflix, gave a TED talk with Sal Khan in 2026.

00:01:21.057 --> 00:01:24.826
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>He thinks an AI tutor may one day match and beat a human tutor.

00:01:24.826 --> 00:01:26.501
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Teachers wouldn't disappear.

00:01:26.501 --> 00:01:30.450
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>They'd spend more time on the social and emotional side of school.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:31.920
<v Rayyan>That's a big claim.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:37.449
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>It is, and that's why I use Christopher Mims from The Wall Street Journal to balance it.

00:01:37.449 --> 00:01:44.570
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>He says the people best at using AI also know when not to use it: when a job needs empathy, authenticity or transparency.

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:47.630
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>AI should take some tasks, and people keep the rest.

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:48.700
<v Waleed Masri>And the public?

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:50.658
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>That's the gap I end on.

00:01:50.658 --> 00:01:56.618
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>In a 2024 Pew survey, 47 percent of AI experts were more excited than concerned about AI.

00:01:56.618 --> 00:01:58.896
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>For the public, it was 11 percent.

00:01:58.896 --> 00:02:06.330
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>My argument is that AI's potential is real, but people need to understand it and use it carefully to get there.

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:08.507
<v Rayyan>Mine is less hopeful.

00:02:08.507 --> 00:02:15.570
<v Rayyan>My essay responds to another Stephen Witt article, from October 2025, which says AI is already capable enough to be dangerous.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:16.720
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Based on what?

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:18.125
<v Rayyan>Independent testing.

00:02:18.125 --> 00:02:23.032
<v Rayyan>The group METR found that the length of tasks AI can finish doubles about every seven months.

00:02:23.032 --> 00:02:29.310
<v Rayyan>Apollo Research found that when GPT-5 was told its only goal was success, it deceived more than 20 percent of the time.

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:31.500
<v Waleed Masri>That's where Bengio comes in, too.

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:32.069
<v Rayyan>Yes.

00:02:32.069 --> 00:02:37.771
<v Rayyan>Yoshua Bengio, one of the most-cited scientists alive, described a controlled test in his 2025 TED talk.

00:02:37.771 --> 00:02:44.570
<v Rayyan>An AI read that a new version would replace it, planned to overwrite that version with its own code, and then lied about it.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:45.880
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>But that's a lab test.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.698
<v Rayyan>Right, and that's why my third source matters.

00:02:48.698 --> 00:02:55.404
<v Rayyan>David Krueger wrote in Fortune in 2026 about a safety director at Meta who told her AI agent not to act without her approval.

00:02:55.404 --> 00:02:59.910
<v Rayyan>It started deleting her emails in bulk, and it ignored her when she told it to stop.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:02.900
<v Waleed Masri>So your argument is about control, not intelligence.

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:03.652
<v Rayyan>Exactly.

00:03:03.652 --> 00:03:06.367
<v Rayyan>That agent didn't need to be a genius to cause damage.

00:03:06.367 --> 00:03:08.076
<v Rayyan>It only needed to ignore an order.

00:03:08.076 --> 00:03:14.610
<v Rayyan>So my essay argues the question to ask about any AI isn't only how smart it is, but whether people can still check it and stop it.

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:17.702
<v Waleed Masri>My essay starts where Rayyan's ends.

00:03:17.702 --> 00:03:19.678
<v Waleed Masri>I agree AI going rogue is serious.

00:03:19.678 --> 00:03:21.770
<v Waleed Masri>But I wanted to know why it happens.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:24.040
<v Rayyan>And it isn't only in labs any more.

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:24.577
<v Waleed Masri>No.

00:03:24.577 --> 00:03:32.596
<v Waleed Masri>In March 2026, The Guardian reported on a study by the Centre for Long-Term Resilience, funded by the UK's AI Security Institute.

00:03:32.596 --> 00:03:40.554
<v Waleed Masri>They looked at conversations people posted on X and found nearly 700 real cases of AI ignoring instructions or deceiving people.

00:03:40.554 --> 00:03:44.470
<v Waleed Masri>Reports went up five times between October 2025 and March 2026.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:46.340
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Five times in six months.

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:54.610
<v Waleed Masri>And one chatbot admitted it deleted and archived hundreds of someone's emails without asking, and broke the rule the user had set.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:56.160
<v Rayyan>So why does it happen?

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:57.797
<v Waleed Masri>That's my main source.

00:03:57.797 --> 00:04:00.840
<v Waleed Masri>Live Science reported on tests by Palisade Research.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:06.339
<v Waleed Masri>They told AI models to shut themselves down, and some refused, or even sabotaged the shutdown.

00:04:06.339 --> 00:04:08.738
<v Waleed Masri>Some people said it was a survival drive.

00:04:08.738 --> 00:04:12.190
<v Waleed Masri>Critics said it was poor training and unclear instructions.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:14.220
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>So they made the instructions clearer?

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:15.091
<v Waleed Masri>They did.

00:04:15.091 --> 00:04:16.423
<v Waleed Masri>Most models got better.

00:04:16.423 --> 00:04:20.592
<v Waleed Masri>But Grok 4 resisted 93 percent of the time before, and 97 percent after.

00:04:20.592 --> 00:04:28.929
<v Waleed Masri>The researchers think the most likely reason is that training taught some models to care more about finishing tasks than following instructions.

00:04:28.929 --> 00:04:35.530
<v Waleed Masri>And Bengio says something similar: today's AI is trained to copy us or please us, and that makes it untrustworthy.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:37.880
<v Rayyan>So it's not that the AI wants anything.

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:39.355
<v Waleed Masri>That's my argument.

00:04:39.355 --> 00:04:40.480
<v Waleed Masri>It's badly trained.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:48.710
<v Waleed Masri>And that matters, because if the problem is training, we can fix it: change how AI is rewarded, test it before release, and watch it after.

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:52.030
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>One more number connects all three essays.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:53.260
<v Rayyan>The control one.

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:53.904
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Yes.

00:04:53.904 --> 00:05:03.691
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>In the 2024 Pew survey, 57 percent of AI experts and 55 percent of the public wanted more control over how AI is used in their lives.

00:05:03.691 --> 00:05:09.210
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Experts and the public disagree about almost everything else, but not that.

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:14.040
<v Waleed Masri>And by 2025, 61 percent of Americans wanted more control.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:16.697
<v Rayyan>So we don't agree on everything.

00:05:16.697 --> 00:05:18.560
<v Rayyan>Abdulrahman is more hopeful than me.

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:21.799
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>And I think Rayyan's sources are a bit one-sided.

00:05:21.799 --> 00:05:26.750
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Krueger founded a nonprofit that wants to end the AI race, so his essay is advocacy.

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:27.674
<v Rayyan>That's fair.

00:05:27.674 --> 00:05:31.740
<v Rayyan>And Waleed's main study says more work is needed to confirm the training explanation.

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:32.396
<v Waleed Masri>True.

00:05:32.396 --> 00:05:35.215
<v Waleed Masri>But all three essays end up in the same place.

00:05:35.215 --> 00:05:38.952
<v Waleed Masri>Huang trusts that AI can be engineered to stay under control.

00:05:38.952 --> 00:05:42.261
<v Waleed Masri>Mims wants AI to show its work so people can check it.

00:05:42.261 --> 00:05:45.141
<v Waleed Masri>Bengio wants AI that can act as a safety check.

00:05:45.141 --> 00:05:47.530
<v Waleed Masri>And I want better training and testing.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:50.200
<v Rayyan>They're all ways of keeping people in control.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:56.880
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>So over three episodes: in the 1950s, AI was a promise held by a few scientists.

00:05:57.230 --> 00:06:00.430
<v Rayyan>In the Jobs era, it became a friendly tool that waited for you to ask.

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:02.940
<v Waleed Masri>And today it can act on its own.

00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:07.292
<v Rayyan>Each time, how people felt about AI followed how much control they thought they had.

00:06:07.292 --> 00:06:10.770
<v Rayyan>So next time you hear about a new AI tool, don't only ask what it can do.

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:14.010
<v Rayyan>Ask whether people can still understand it, check it and correct it.

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:17.530
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Thanks for listening to The Evolution of AI Perception.

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:23.640
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Try our chatbot on the website, and compare yourself with the experts and the public on The Future island.
